  "note": "The continuity path remainder stays PROPOSED; every operation carries its own standing, which replaced the former built_operations list. This note used to open with a count of the BUILT ones, and it was wrong; the correction of it quoted the old note saying something it never said; the correction of that miscounted again. Three consecutive commits, three independent observations, and nothing in the repository reads this field. So it states no counts: the operations array carries them and cannot drift from itself. Every BUILT operation checks the authority contracts/capability-offices.json declares for it: the nine that declared one and checked none were guarded on Bdo's ruling of 2026-08-25, so live_matching_grant and NO_LIVE_GRANT are declared on each. Every operation that names a record also checks the record belongs to this node - a grant bound to its minting node stops a permit crossing, not a node reading another node's records under a permit of its own - so local_node_record is declared on every operation that names a record by id - `revoke` among them since 2026-08-26, which enforced the check and declared `grant_exists` alone - and on `list-publications` and `session-context`, which name no record and answer from a fold already narrowed to this node. Nothing in the repository compares a declared precondition against the check that implements it, which is how `revoke` came to enforce a check it did not declare; that gap belongs to the contracts domain and is reported, not closed here. FOREIGN_NODE_RECORD is declared only on the three that check the node after the caller has shown a grant; the rest read the record to find their grant's scope, so they answer a foreign record exactly as they answer a missing one. That answer is UNKNOWN_RECORD, which the service produced from eight operations and declared nowhere until 2026-08-26, while the routed read in bindings/mcp answered the same fact about the same thread with THREAD_UNKNOWN. One fact carries one code: THREAD_UNKNOWN is retired, UNKNOWN_RECORD is declared on the eight operations that can return it, and it maps to the kernel's MISSING_PRECONDITION because the *_exists precondition each of them declares is what failed. services/console/tests/test_declared_refusals.py drives every one of those operations against an absent id and fails if a produced code is undeclared. Four operations that have enforced authority since before 2026-08-25 never declared NO_LIVE_GRANT, and PIN_INCOMPLETE, SESSION_CLOSED, THREAD_ARCHIVED and CLAIM_WITHOUT_PROPOSAL were raised without appearing in local_refusals at all. discover-operations also declares UNREADABLE and capability_map_readable: it read the projection's keys on faith, so a map that is valid JSON and the wrong shape - or a file that is absent or unparseable - came back through the CLI's catch-all as UNKNOWN_RECORD, undeclared here and wrong besides, since nothing was missing from the journal. UNREADABLE is the kernel's own code for a source that cannot be read, borrowed rather than synonymised. Declared 2026-08-25 and 2026-08-26. scripts/sovkernel/manifests.py checks that a declared refusal is known and never that a produced one is declared, so nothing in the shared gate catches this class repository-wide; that gap belongs to the contracts domain and is reported, not closed here. Two questions this file cannot answer about itself, raised by an independent observation on 2026-08-26 and held for Bdo. First: does `preconditions` claim what an operation *enforces*, or describe its logical shape? Under the first reading eight console operations declare `session_live` and do not check it - only `posts.py` and `routes.py` do - and `console.discover-operations` therefore tells a fresh participant that `console.grant` needs a session, in the same run in which `console.grant` commits without one. Under the second reading the `local_node_record` added to `revoke` repaired nothing. Both readings cannot stand, the answer changes ten manifests rather than this one, and nothing in the repository compares a declared precondition against the check implementing it. The four operations that enforced an authority and declared no precondition for it - archive-thread, open-channel, open-thread, post - now declare live_matching_grant, which is right under either reading. Second: is a note a governed claim held to the standard of the fields beside it? Nothing reads this one."
}
